/T (In the above example, the assign operator knows that the value assigned to y is a primitive type \(number type in this case\), so when the second line code executes, where the value of y is assigned to z, the assign operator takes the value of y \(234\) and allocates a new space in the memory and returns the address. Therefore, variable z is not pointing to the location of variable y, instead, it is pointing to a new location in the memory.)
/E (In the above example, the assign operator knows that the value assigned to y is a primitive type \(number type in this case\), so when the second line code executes, where the value of y is assigned to z, the assign operator takes the value of y \(234\) and allocates a new space in the memory and returns the address. Therefore, variable z is not pointing to the location of variable y, instead, it is pointing to a new location in the memory.)
